  "id": "DrugBank_DB13679 - MONDO_10481",
  "verbalization": "Dexchlorpheniramine, a second-generation antihistamine, is implicated in the development of angioedema through a chain of indications involving urticaria. The evidence suggests that Dexchlorpheniramine may contribute to angioedema via a mechanism that involves the induction of urticaria, which in turn is linked to angioedema through the actions of other drugs such as bilastine and rupatadine. These drugs are also antihistamines, and their association with angioedema implies a potential shared mechanism, possibly involving the modulation of histamine release or the activation of mast cell degranulation. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The presence of multiple antihistamine drugs with similar indications for angioedema suggests that the path may be biologically plausible, but further investigation is needed to confirm the specific role of Dexchlorpheniramine in this process.",
